        'Standard deviation is the most important statistical concept in finance. It measures how much investment returns deviate from the average, the wider the spread, the higher the risk. The S&P 500 has a historical standard deviation of approximately 15% annually, meaning that in a typical year, returns fall within ±15% of the 10.3% mean (so roughly between -5% and +25%) about 68% of the time.',
        'Variance is simply standard deviation squared. While standard deviation is expressed in the same units as returns (percentage points), variance is in squared units, making it harder to interpret directly but essential in portfolio mathematics. The S&P 500\'s variance is approximately 225 (15% squared). Modern Portfolio Theory, developed by Harry Markowitz (Nobel Prize 1990), uses variance as the primary measure of portfolio risk.',
