      'LTCM\'s failure teaches the single most important lesson in financial statistics: the normal distribution is an approximation, not reality. Real financial returns have "fat tails", extreme events occur 5-10x more often than the bell curve predicts. LTCM\'s models were mathematically elegant but assumed that market behavior in calm times would persist in crises. Standard deviation is essential for measuring typical risk, but it systematically underestimates the probability of catastrophic events. Every model is wrong, the question is whether you know how it\'s wrong.'
