      'Correct! Despite winning 95% of the time, the expected value is -$300 per trade. This is exactly how LTCM operated, they won on most trades but the rare catastrophic losses wiped out all gains and more. Expected value, not win rate, determines long-term profitability. Casinos use this same math: they lose on individual bets all the time but maintain a positive expected value across millions of bets.',
      'The expected value is (0.95 x $1,000) + (0.05 x -$25,000) = $950 - $1,250 = -$300. Despite a 95% win rate, the magnitude of the 5% loss makes this a losing proposition. This is why expected value, not probability of winning, is the correct decision metric.'
