        'Value at Risk (VaR) answers the question: "What is the maximum I could lose in a given time period with a certain confidence level?" A 95% daily VaR of $1 million means there is a 5% chance of losing more than $1 million in a single day. JPMorgan\'s RiskMetrics group popularized VaR in the 1990s, and it became the standard risk measure required by banking regulators under Basel III. However, VaR has a critical flaw: it tells you the threshold of your losses but not how bad losses could be beyond that threshold.',
        'The Sharpe ratio, developed by Nobel laureate William Sharpe in 1966, measures risk-adjusted return: (Portfolio Return - Risk-Free Rate) / Portfolio Standard Deviation. A Sharpe ratio of 1.0 means you earn 1% excess return for every 1% of volatility. The S&P 500 has a historical Sharpe ratio of approximately 0.4. Renaissance Technologies\' Medallion Fund achieved an estimated Sharpe ratio above 2.0, extraordinary by any standard. Higher Sharpe ratios indicate more efficient use of risk.',
        'Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR), also called Expected Shortfall, fixes VaR\'s blind spot by asking: "When losses exceed the VaR threshold, what is the average loss?" If 95% VaR is -$1 million but CVaR is -$3 million, you know that the 5% worst-case scenarios average three times the VaR threshold. After the 2008 crisis, regulators increasingly require CVaR alongside VaR because it captures tail risk that VaR ignores, the same tail risk that destroyed LTCM.'
