          truth: 'This is the gambler\'s fallacy. Each day\'s return is largely independent of previous days. A coin doesn\'t "remember" past flips, and markets don\'t "owe" you a reversal. Momentum research actually shows stocks that have risen tend to keep rising in the short term.',
          explanation: 'The gambler\'s fallacy confuses the probability of a sequence with the probability of the next event. The probability of 10 consecutive up days is low beforehand, but given that 9 have already occurred, the 10th day\'s probability is still roughly 50/50. Momentum strategies exploit this, they buy stocks that have been rising, not sell them.'
