            { label: 'Keep VaR as the sole risk measure, it\'s the industry standard', consequence: 'VaR alone missed the 2008 crisis. Banks relying only on VaR saw actual losses 5-10x their VaR estimates during the crisis. The Basel III reforms specifically added Expected Shortfall requirements because VaR alone is insufficient.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'Replace VaR entirely with Monte Carlo simulation', consequence: 'Monte Carlo is powerful but computationally expensive for daily use. The best approach combines multiple risk measures. Monte Carlo is excellent for scenario analysis but VaR and CVaR provide quick daily metrics.', score: 1 },
            { label: 'Implement a multi-layered system: VaR for daily monitoring, CVaR (Expected Shortfall) for tail-risk measurement, stress tests for crisis scenarios, and Monte Carlo for comprehensive scenario analysis', consequence: 'Excellent. This layered approach addresses every known failure mode. VaR provides a quick daily threshold, CVaR reveals tail severity, stress tests model specific crisis scenarios, and Monte Carlo maps the full distribution of outcomes. This is the modern best practice adopted after 2008.', score: 3 }
