            { label: 'Use the highest historical correlation (crisis periods) as the permanent assumption', consequence: 'Using only crisis correlations is too conservative, it would make every portfolio look extremely risky and prevent reasonable investing. The solution is to model correlation regimes, not use a single fixed estimate.', score: 1 },
            { label: 'Build a regime-switching model: use calm-market correlations (0.3) for normal VaR, but stress-test with crisis correlations (0.8-0.95) from 1998, 2008, and 2020. Apply Bayesian updating to shift between regimes as market conditions change', consequence: 'This is the gold standard. Regime-switching models acknowledge that markets behave fundamentally differently in calm vs. crisis periods. Bayesian updating allows the model to detect early warning signals and shift to crisis-mode correlations before full damage occurs. This is what modern quantitative risk management looks like.', score: 3 }
