            { label: 'Present the Fama-French findings showing zero alpha after factor adjustment. Recommend replacing high-fee active positions with low-cost factor ETFs that provide the same size and value exposure at 0.1% fees, saving approximately $35 million annually in unnecessary costs', consequence: 'Outstanding. The regression evidence is clear: there is no genuine alpha, only factor exposure disguised as skill. Switching to factor ETFs maintains the same return drivers at a fraction of the cost. This is regression analysis creating direct, measurable value for the fund\'s beneficiaries. Annual savings of $35M compound enormously over a pension fund\'s multi-decade horizon.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Ignore the regression results, the managers have good track records', consequence: 'Track records without regression adjustment are meaningless. A manager who loads on small-cap value stocks will look like a genius during periods when those factors perform well, that\'s not skill, it\'s exposure. Regression is the only way to separate factor returns from genuine alpha.', score: 0 }
