            { label: 'Demand out-of-sample testing, walk-forward validation, and a reduction to fewer than 10 theoretically justified variables. Reject unless the model proves predictive on data it was never trained on', consequence: 'This is the correct analytical response. 80 variables guarantees overfitting. Demanding out-of-sample testing (testing on data the model has never seen) is the gold standard. Walk-forward validation simulates real-time decision-making. And requiring theoretical justification for each variable prevents data mining. Any model that survives these tests deserves consideration; most don\'t.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Ask them to add more variables to get accuracy to 99%', consequence: 'More variables increases overfitting, not accuracy. Each additional variable fits more historical noise while reducing out-of-sample predictive power. The goal is the simplest model that captures genuine relationships, not the most complex model that memorizes history.', score: 0 }
