            { label: 'Run regressions on each holding to identify high-beta stocks, then systematically replace them with lower-beta alternatives in the same sectors, maintaining sector diversification while reducing aggregate beta from 1.4 to 1.0', consequence: 'Excellent regression-based approach. By regressing each stock against the market, you can identify which holdings contribute most to portfolio beta. Replacing high-beta stocks (beta 2.0+) with lower-beta alternatives (beta 0.5-0.8) in the same sectors reduces market sensitivity while preserving diversification and sector exposure. This is surgical, regression-driven portfolio management.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Ignore beta, it changes over time anyway so it\'s useless', consequence: 'While beta does change over time, it remains the best single measure of market sensitivity. The fact that it evolves means you should recalculate regularly (rolling regression), not ignore it. A portfolio with beta 1.4 heading into a market downturn carries 40% more systematic risk than the market, that\'s highly relevant.', score: 1 }
