      ['Why must all three financial statements be linked?','Because they describe different aspects of the same business. Net income (IS) feeds retained earnings (BS). Balance sheet changes feed the cash flow statement. Cash from the CF statement links back to the BS. Without linkage, changing one assumption requires manual updates everywhere, breeding errors and inconsistencies.'],
      ['What is the most common type of financial model error?','Hard-coded numbers in formulas. Instead of =B5*TaxRate (referencing a labeled assumption), analysts write =B5*0.21 (burying the tax rate). When the tax rate changes, these hidden numbers aren\'t updated. The London Whale\'s formula error was a variant of this, a mechanical error that went undetected because the model lacked transparency.'],
      ['Why do investment banks color-code their models?','Blue for input assumptions, black for formulas, green for links to other sheets. This convention lets any analyst quickly identify what can be changed (blue inputs) versus what is calculated (black formulas). It makes model auditing possible and dramatically reduces errors during the review process.'],
      ['How does sensitivity analysis protect against model uncertainty?','By showing the range of outcomes across different assumptions. If a company is worth $50-$150 per share depending on reasonable assumption ranges, that\'s crucial information. A single "point estimate" of $100 creates false precision. Sensitivity analysis reveals that the real answer is a range, not a number.'],
      ['What was the London Whale\'s specific model error?','The VaR model used to measure risk divided by the sum of two numbers instead of their average. This effectively halved the reported risk, making JPMorgan\'s $100+ billion position appear far safer than it was. The error went undetected because the model was not independently verified with sufficient rigor.'],
      ['Why is free cash flow more reliable than net income?','Net income can be manipulated through accounting choices (depreciation methods, revenue recognition, asset valuation). Cash flow is harder to fake, either cash entered the bank account or it didn\'t. Enron manipulated net income for years but couldn\'t hide the cash flow discrepancy. "Cash is fact; profit is opinion."']
