            { label: 'Jump straight to the valuation, calculate what the company is worth', consequence: 'Valuation without a foundation is meaningless. A DCF requires free cash flow projections, which require an income statement, which requires revenue assumptions, which require industry research. Starting at the end produces garbage-in-garbage-out results.', score: 0 },
            { label: 'Gather 5 years of historical financial statements, identify key drivers (revenue growth, margins, capex, working capital), build a dedicated assumptions page, then project the income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow statement in sequence', consequence: 'This is the gold-standard approach at every investment bank. Historical data reveals trends. Key drivers quantify the levers. A dedicated assumptions page ensures transparency. Sequential three-statement construction ensures integrity. Only after all three statements are linked should you build the valuation.', score: 3 },
            { label: 'Copy a competitor\'s model and change the company name', consequence: 'Different companies have different drivers, margins, and capital structures. A model built for a software company won\'t work for a manufacturer. Each model must be built from the specific company\'s historical financials and industry dynamics.', score: 0 }
