    cq('JPMorgan\'s London Whale model contained a formula error that divided by a sum instead of an average. What was the practical impact of this single Excel error?',
      ['It made no material difference, because risk models are robust enough to absorb a single bad formula','It made positions appear approximately half as risky as they actually were, contributing to $6.2 billion in losses, the largest single trading loss in banking history','It doubled the apparent risk, causing the bank to trade far too conservatively and miss out on profit','It only affected the model\'s formatting and never touched any of the actual risk calculations'],
      1,'Dividing by the sum (instead of the average) of two numbers doubles the denominator, halving the result. In a risk model, this made JPMorgan\'s massive credit derivative positions appear 50% less risky than they were. Traders maintained and expanded positions based on the flawed risk readings. When the market moved against them, losses were catastrophic. A single formula error, amplified through the model, contributed to the largest trading loss ever.'),
    cq('Bethany McLean exposed Enron\'s $74 billion fraud using a surprisingly simple analytical technique. What was it?',
      ['Advanced machine learning algorithms that scanned thousands of corporate filings for hidden fraud signals','Comparing Enron\'s income statement to its cash flow statement, profits were growing rapidly but operating cash flow was declining, revealing that earnings were manufactured through accounting tricks rather than real business activity','Insider tips from Enron employees who quietly warned her that the company\'s books were fabricated','Technical analysis of Enron\'s stock chart, where a clear bearish pattern signalled the coming collapse'],
      1,'McLean\'s insight was beautifully simple: if a company is genuinely profitable, cash should be flowing in. Enron reported growing profits on its income statement while operating cash flow declined. This divergence meant profits were being created through accounting maneuvers (mark-to-market, SPE transactions) rather than actual business activity. The three-statement comparison, income statement vs. cash flow statement, remains the most powerful fraud detection tool available to any analyst.'),
    cq('A financial model shows a company is worth $100 per share at 15% growth, but only $40 per share at 8% growth. What does this demonstrate?',
      ['The model is broken, because a correctly built model should return one answer rather than a range','This demonstrates the critical importance of sensitivity analysis, a single assumption (growth rate) changes the valuation by 2.5x. Any investment decision must be evaluated across a range of assumptions, not at a single point estimate','You should always pick the higher growth rate, since the more optimistic case is the one worth pursuing','The lower valuation is always the accurate one, because conservative assumptions are never wrong'],
      1,'A 2.5x valuation swing from a single assumption change reveals that the model\'s output is highly sensitive to growth rate. This is exactly why sensitivity analysis exists: it forces decision-makers to consider the range of outcomes rather than anchoring to a single "precise" number. If you\'re paying $80 per share, the investment works at 15% growth but loses 50% at 8% growth, critical information for making an informed decision.'),
    cq('Why is comparing the income statement to the cash flow statement considered the best "fraud detector" in financial analysis?',
      ['Because the income statement is always more accurate, so any gap means the cash flow statement is wrong','Because net income can be manipulated through accounting choices (revenue recognition, depreciation, asset valuation) but cash flow is harder to fake, either money entered the bank account or it didn\'t. When profits grow without corresponding cash growth, earnings may be manufactured','Because the cash flow statement is always unreliable and exists only as a regulatory formality','Because the two statements should always show identical numbers, so any difference signals fraud'],
      1,'Net income is based on accounting rules that offer significant discretion: when to recognize revenue, how to depreciate assets, how to value inventory. These choices can inflate reported profits. Cash flow, however, reflects actual money movement, you either received cash or you didn\'t. Enron manipulated net income for years through mark-to-market accounting and SPE transactions, but couldn\'t hide the deteriorating cash flow. "Cash is fact; profit is opinion."'),
    cq('The European Spreadsheet Risks Interest Group found that 88% of spreadsheets contain errors. Why are financial model errors particularly dangerous?',
      ['Spreadsheet errors are harmless in finance, because analysts always catch them before any decision','Financial model errors compound through interconnected statements, a single formula error can cascade through the income statement, balance sheet, and cash flow statement, producing valuations that are off by 50% or more. The London Whale\'s copy-paste error contributed to $6.2 billion in losses','Errors reliably cancel each other out in large models, so the final valuation stays roughly correct','Only small companies use spreadsheets, so the 88% error rate never reaches major banks or funds'],
      1,'In a three-statement model, a revenue assumption error flows through cost of goods sold, operating income, net income, retained earnings, working capital, and free cash flow, compounding at each step. The London Whale\'s formula error halved the reported risk, enabling positions to grow to catastrophic size. This is why investment banks require independent formula verification, color-coded inputs, and no hard-coded numbers, architectural rules designed to catch errors before they compound into billions in losses.')
