      {label:'Agree and increase growth assumptions until the model matches the market price',outcome:'Reverse-engineering assumptions to match the market price defeats the entire purpose of DCF. If you adjust inputs until the output matches the market, you\'re not valuing, you\'re rationalizing. This is exactly what dotcom analysts did before the bubble burst.',correct:false},
      {label:'Present the DCF showing $50/share and run a reverse DCF to show what growth assumptions the $120 price implies. If those implied assumptions are unrealistic (e.g., 40% growth for 20 years), the stock is overvalued regardless of what the market "knows." The market can be irrational, Pets.com and dotcom stocks proved that conclusively',outcome:'Excellent. The reverse DCF reveals the market\'s implied expectations. If $120 requires 40% growth for 20 years, making the company larger than the entire industry, the price is detached from fundamentals. Buffett says: "In the short run, the market is a voting machine. In the long run, it is a weighing machine." DCF is the scale.',correct:true},
      {label:'Abandon DCF and use only market sentiment for valuation',outcome:'Market sentiment is what creates bubbles. The Nasdaq fell 78% from 2000-2002 precisely because investors abandoned fundamental analysis for sentiment. DCF exists to anchor valuation in reality when markets become irrational.',correct:false}
