      { term: 'Candlestick Chart', definition: 'A financial chart type where each time period is shown as a "candle" with a body (open-to-close range) and wicks (high and low), with color indicating price direction. Invented by Japanese rice trader Munehisa Homma in the 1700s.', example: 'A daily Tesla candlestick: open $240, high $252, low $238, close $248, shown as a green candle with body spanning $240-$248 and wicks to $238 and $252.', sentence: 'The trader identified a "doji" candlestick pattern, where the open and close were nearly identical, signaling market indecision before a major breakout.' },
      { term: 'Heatmap', definition: 'A visualization using color intensity across a grid to represent values, commonly used in finance for correlation matrices, sector performance, and risk exposure analysis.', example: 'A 10-sector correlation heatmap showing tech and consumer discretionary at 0.82 (deep red) while tech and utilities show 0.12 (pale green), instantly revealing diversification opportunities.', sentence: 'The portfolio manager used a heatmap to identify that three of her top holdings had correlations above 0.9, essentially tripling her concentration risk.' },
      { term: 'Data-Ink Ratio', definition: 'Edward Tufte\'s principle measuring the proportion of a chart\'s visual elements that display actual data versus decoration. Higher ratios indicate more efficient, informative visualizations.', example: 'Bloomberg Terminal: nearly 100% data-ink ratio with prices, charts, and metrics filling the screen. A typical infographic: often below 30% data-ink ratio with illustrations and whitespace dominating.', sentence: 'After removing gridlines, 3D effects, and the decorative border, the chart\'s data-ink ratio improved from 0.4 to 0.85, making the revenue trend immediately obvious.' },
      { term: 'Treemap', definition: 'A space-filling visualization where nested rectangles represent hierarchical data, with rectangle size proportional to a quantitative variable. Ideal for portfolio composition and market structure.', example: 'The S&P 500 treemap on finviz.com shows each stock as a rectangle sized by market cap and colored by daily performance, Apple\'s large green rectangle immediately communicates "biggest stock, up today."', sentence: 'The analyst used a treemap to show the client that 65% of their portfolio was concentrated in just three sectors, making the need for diversification visually obvious.' },
      { term: 'KPI Dashboard', definition: 'A visual display showing Key Performance Indicators, the most critical metrics for monitoring financial performance, arranged with visual hierarchy so the most important information is seen first.', example: 'A hedge fund dashboard showing: Total AUM ($2.3B), YTD Return (+14.2%), Sharpe Ratio (1.8), Max Drawdown (-6.1%), and Net Exposure (72%), all updated in real-time.', sentence: 'The CFO redesigned the company\'s KPI dashboard to show cash runway at the top in large font, after realizing the previous layout buried this critical metric below less important revenue charts.' },
      { term: 'Lie Factor', definition: 'Edward Tufte\'s metric measuring chart distortion: the size of the visual effect divided by the size of the actual data effect. A lie factor of 1.0 means no distortion; anything above 1.05 indicates misleading design.', example: 'A chart with Y-axis from 98 to 102 showing data from 99 to 101: the 2% actual change fills 100% of the chart height, creating a lie factor of ~50.', sentence: 'The analyst flagged the competitor\'s investor presentation for having a lie factor of 8, their revenue chart truncated the Y-axis to make 5% growth look like a dramatic surge.' }
