    cq('Renaissance Technologies\' Medallion Fund averaged 66% annual returns from 1988-2018. What was unique about their hiring approach?',
      ['They recruited the top MBA graduates from Wharton and Harvard Business School, betting that the strongest formal finance education available would give them an unbeatable edge in trading the markets','They hired mathematicians, physicists, and computer scientists, explicitly avoiding traditional finance professionals, because Jim Simons believed pattern recognition skills from math and science translated to financial markets better than finance training','They only hired experienced former Goldman Sachs traders, on the theory that years spent on a major trading floor was the single best preparation for running a quantitative hedge fund','They did not hire investment staff at all, because the fund\'s artificial intelligence ran itself completely and needed no human team to design, test, or supervise its trading'],
      1,'Jim Simons, himself a former NSA codebreaker and math professor, built Renaissance by recruiting from academia and government labs, not Wall Street. The team includes former string theorists, computational linguists, and signal processing experts. Simons\' insight was that finding patterns in noisy financial data is fundamentally a math and physics problem, not a finance problem.'),
    cq('Over 90% of ML trading strategies that look profitable in backtesting fail when deployed with real money. What is the primary cause?',
      ['The computers used in live trading are too slow to keep up with real-time prices, so the strategy that worked in a calm backtest cannot execute fast enough once markets are moving','Overfitting, the models memorize random noise in historical data rather than learning genuine patterns, so they perform perfectly on past data but fail on unseen future data','The overall stock market shifted between the backtest period and the live period, so the strategy was sound but simply faced a different market than the one it was built on','Trading fees and commissions quietly eat up all of the strategy\'s profits, turning a backtest that looked strongly positive into a small loss once real transaction costs are applied'],
      1,'Overfitting is the fundamental challenge in financial ML. It\'s easy to find patterns in any historical dataset, but most of those patterns are noise. A model achieving 200% backtested returns may just be memorizing that "on March 15, 2019, this specific combination of 47 features predicted a 2% gain", a coincidence that will never repeat. Proper validation (out-of-sample testing) is the only defense.'),
    cq('Goldman Sachs had 600 equity traders in 2000 and just 2 by 2017. But they added 200 engineers. What does this tell us about ML\'s impact on finance?',
      ['Machine learning is eliminating jobs in finance across the board, and the Goldman numbers show that the industry is steadily shrinking its total workforce as algorithms take over one role after another','ML automates execution and pattern recognition (replacing traders) but creates new roles in model building, monitoring, and adaptation (requiring engineers). The total transformation is a shift in required skills, not a simple reduction in headcount','Engineers are simply cheaper to employ than traders, so Goldman swapped one group for the other purely to cut its payroll costs while keeping the same trading operation running','Goldman Sachs is heading out of business, since losing nearly all of its equity traders is a clear sign that the firm can no longer compete in the modern, fully automated market'],
      1,'The Goldman example is a microcosm of ML\'s impact across finance. Repetitive, rule-based tasks (trade execution, document processing, basic analysis) are automated. But this creates demand for new skills: ML engineers, data scientists, and quantitative researchers who build and maintain the algorithms. JPMorgan now employs 50,000+ technologists. The jobs didn\'t disappear, they transformed.'),
    cq('JPMorgan\'s COiN (Contract Intelligence) platform uses NLP to analyze 12,000 commercial loan agreements in seconds. What did this task previously require?',
      ['About 12,000 hours of computer processing time, since the same agreements still had to be scanned and parsed by older software that simply ran far more slowly than the COiN system','360,000 hours of human lawyer work per year, COiN uses NLP to extract 150 key attributes from each document automatically, replacing manual review with ML-powered document analysis','A small team of about 12 interns, who worked through the loan agreements by hand each year before the bank built the COiN platform to take over that repetitive review','Nothing comparable was ever done before COiN, because reviewing thousands of loan agreements for key terms was a task the bank simply never attempted until the software existed'],
      1,'COiN demonstrates NLP\'s transformative power in finance. Each loan agreement contains hundreds of clauses, conditions, and risk factors that lawyers previously reviewed manually. COiN\'s NLP models extract 150 key attributes per document in seconds, processing the entire annual workflow in minutes. This freed lawyers to focus on complex judgment calls while ML handled repetitive extraction, a classic example of human+ML collaboration.'),
    cq('A startup claims its ML model "predicts stock prices with 95% accuracy." Based on what you\'ve learned about ML in finance, what is the most likely reality?',
      ['They have achieved a genuine breakthrough in financial AI, and a 95% accuracy model is exactly the kind of edge that would let a startup quietly outperform every established hedge fund in the world','The 95% figure is almost certainly due to overfitting (testing on training data), data leakage (accidentally including future information in training), or a misleading definition of "accuracy", even the best quant funds achieve only 51-55% directional accuracy per trade','A 95% accuracy figure is completely standard for modern machine learning models, so there is nothing unusual about the claim and the startup is simply reporting a normal, expected result','They are using quantum computing, and quantum hardware is what makes 95% stock prediction accuracy possible, since classical computers could never process market data fast enough to reach that level'],
      1,'A 95% accuracy claim for stock prediction is a massive red flag. Financial markets are adaptive systems, when a pattern is discovered, trading on it erases the pattern. Even Renaissance Technologies\' Medallion Fund, the most successful quant strategy in history, achieves only marginal edges above 50/50. The most common explanations for 95% claims: overfitting (model tested on training data), data leakage (future info leaked into features), or defining "accuracy" as something trivial (like predicting price stays within ±10%).')
