        'Financial analytics has created an array of career paths that didn\'t exist 20 years ago. Quantitative Analysts ("quants") build mathematical models for trading, risk management, and derivatives pricing, commanding starting salaries of $150,000-$250,000 at firms like Citadel, Two Sigma, and Jane Street. Data Scientists in finance apply ML and statistical methods to credit scoring, fraud detection, and customer analytics, with median salaries of $130,000-$180,000 at major banks. Financial analysts use DCF models, regression, and visualization to evaluate investments, starting at $80,000-$120,000 and growing to $200,000+ as portfolio managers.',
        'The most explosive growth area is at the intersection of finance and technology ("fintech"). Roles like ML Engineers for trading systems, NLP specialists for document analysis, and dashboard developers for financial platforms are in extreme demand. JPMorgan alone employs 50,000+ technologists. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects 9% growth for financial analysts and 35% growth for data scientists through 2032, but these numbers understate the trend because many new roles (AI risk officer, algorithmic audit specialist) didn\'t exist when the projections were made.',
        'What all these paths share is a foundation in the skills covered in this course: descriptive statistics, probability, regression, modeling, Monte Carlo simulation, data visualization, and machine learning fundamentals. Whether you become a quant building trading algorithms, an analyst evaluating companies, or a fintech entrepreneur building the next Bloomberg Terminal, the analytical workflow is the same: collect data → analyze statistically → model scenarios → visualize clearly → decide based on evidence. Goldman Sachs replacing 600 traders with 200 engineers wasn\'t an anomaly, it was a preview of every financial institution\'s future.'
