      [
        { date: 'Month 1', event: 'A junior analyst notices a mid-cap cloud computing company trading at seemingly low multiples despite strong growth', context: 'The analyst begins with descriptive statistics: 5-year revenue CAGR of 35%, gross margins expanding from 62% to 78%, and net retention rate of 135% (existing customers spending 35% more each year). Regression analysis shows that revenue growth is the strongest predictor of valuation multiples in the cloud sector, and this company\'s growth rate implies a much higher valuation than its current price.' },
        { date: 'Month 2', event: 'The analyst builds a detailed DCF model and layers Monte Carlo simulation on top', context: 'The base-case DCF estimates intrinsic value at $85/share versus a current price of $55, a 55% margin of safety. But the real insight comes from Monte Carlo: running 10,000 scenarios by varying growth rates, margins, and discount rates shows a 75% probability the stock is worth $65-$140, with only a 10% chance it\'s worth less than $55 (the current price). The risk/reward is asymmetric: limited downside, massive potential upside.' },
        { date: 'Month 3', event: 'NLP analysis of earnings calls and ML clustering reveal additional bullish signals', context: 'NLP sentiment analysis of the CEO\'s last four earnings calls shows increasingly confident language: fewer hedging words ("hope," "believe") and more definitive statements ("we will," "committed to"). ML clustering identifies the company as most similar to Salesforce and ServiceNow at comparable stages in their growth, both of which subsequently tripled in value. The integrated analysis, DCF + Monte Carlo + regression + NLP + clustering, produces a unified thesis: high-conviction buy.' },
        { date: 'Year 3', event: 'The stock reaches $220/share, a 400% return from the $55 entry point', context: 'The investment thesis played out as the integrated analysis predicted: revenue growth continued at 30%+, margins expanded, and the market re-rated the stock to match comparable companies. No single analytical tool would have produced this conviction. The DCF alone showed moderate undervaluation. Monte Carlo showed asymmetric risk/reward. Regression confirmed growth was the key driver. NLP detected management confidence. Clustering identified the right peer group. Together, they built an unassailable case.' }
      ],
      'This case study demonstrates the power of integrated financial analytics. Each tool alone provided partial insight: DCF gave a point estimate, Monte Carlo mapped the probability distribution, regression identified the key value driver, NLP added qualitative evidence, and clustering provided the right comparison framework. The junior analyst\'s edge wasn\'t any single technique, it was the disciplined integration of all of them into a coherent investment thesis. This is exactly the workflow used by the world\'s best financial analysts, and it\'s the workflow you\'ve learned in this course.'
