      ['Accept the single DCF estimate and recommend buying right away, since the $100 value sits well above the $70 price and the wide gap is reason enough to act with confidence','Run Monte Carlo simulation on the DCF, vary growth rates, margins, and discount rates across 10,000 scenarios to produce a probability distribution that shows the range of possible values and the confidence level of the $100 estimate','Have her rebuild the slide deck with cleaner charts, because a more polished presentation of the single $100 estimate is what the manager is really asking the analyst to provide','Ask a senior analyst to redo the entire valuation from scratch, on the assumption that a junior analyst cannot produce a number the desk should rely on for a real buy recommendation'],
      1,
      'A single DCF gives false precision, one estimate that depends entirely on the specific assumptions chosen. Monte Carlo varies those assumptions across thousands of scenarios, producing a distribution: "There\'s a 70% chance the stock is worth $80-$130, a median of $100, and only a 15% chance it\'s worth less than $70." This transforms a guess into a risk-informed recommendation with quantified confidence levels.'
