      {label:'Accept that the market is always right and abandon your analysis',outcome:'The Efficient Market Hypothesis in its strong form claims prices always reflect all information, but if this were true, no investor could ever consistently outperform. Renaissance Technologies\' 66% annual returns and Warren Buffett\'s 50+ year track record prove that mispricings exist. Your integrated analysis found one.',correct:false},
      {label:'Present your integrated evidence: the DCF shows 40% upside, Monte Carlo gives 78% confidence, regression confirms the key growth driver is underappreciated, and NLP shows increasing management confidence, acknowledge uncertainty but explain that the weight of evidence supports the thesis',outcome:'Perfect. You\'re not claiming certainty, you\'re presenting probability-weighted evidence from multiple independent analytical tools. If DCF, Monte Carlo, regression, and NLP all point the same direction, the convergence of evidence is much stronger than any single signal. Acknowledging the 22% probability of being wrong is intellectually honest and distinguishes analysis from speculation.',correct:true},
      {label:'Tell your colleague they\'re outdated and don\'t understand data',outcome:'Dismissing a senior colleague without addressing their valid concern (market efficiency) undermines your credibility. The correct approach is to respectfully present your evidence while acknowledging uncertainty, showing that your analysis is rigorous, not arrogant.',correct:false}
