        { term: 'Signal-to-Noise Ratio', definition: 'The proportion of useful information (signal) relative to irrelevant noise in a dataset. Higher SNR means more reliable predictions.', example: 'Aggregate Twitter sentiment about stocks has a low SNR (~10% signal). Filtering to verified financial accounts raises the SNR to ~40%. Earnings call NLP has ~60% SNR because management language is more structured and information-rich.' }
